---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../styles/pages/privacy.css";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="The LIT Bible privacy policy. Your reading life is your own. Reading needs no account, the site and apps keep what you create on your device, and an optional account syncs your notebook locked with a key we never have."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/privacy/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "name": "LIT Bible Privacy Policy",
      "url": "https://litbible.net/privacy/",
      "dateModified": "2026-11-26",
      "description": "The LIT Bible privacy policy. Your reading life is your own. Reading needs no account, the site and apps keep what you create on your device, and an optional account syncs your notebook locked with a key we never have.",
      "isPartOf": {
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      },
      "publisher": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "LIT Bible",
        "url": "https://litbible.net"
      }
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="privacy-page" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">

    <header class="privacy-header">
      <div class="privacy-header__inner">
        <h1 id="privacy-title" class="privacy-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="privacy-effective">
          Effective date: <time datetime="2026-11-26">November 26, 2026</time>
        </p>
        {/* The "what changed" line src/pages/CLAUDE.md rule 4 promises; it
            comes off after 2026-12-26, 30 days from the later date. */}
        <p class="privacy-effective privacy-changed">
          <strong>What changed</strong> on
          <time datetime="2026-11-25">November 25, 2026</time>: this policy
          now describes the optional LIT Bible account (not open yet) and what
          it keeps, the security reports your browser may send, how to ask
          what we hold wherever you live, and a minimum age for an account.
          Our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a> are new the same day. On
          <time datetime="2026-11-26">November 26, 2026</time>: we named the
          other companies’ players and giving form on two of our pages, made
          four account details exact, and said when an account with no
          notebook is deleted.
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-lede">
          The LIT Bible is built on a simple principle: your reading life is
          your own. This policy describes what data the LIT Bible website
          (litbible.net) collects, how the companion iOS and Android apps
          handle data, and what the optional LIT Bible account keeps.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="privacy-body">

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-short">
        <h2 id="privacy-short">In short</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Reading the LIT needs no account. The website and the apps keep
            what you create on your own device.
          </li>
          <li>
            We collect personal information only when you choose to: when you
            subscribe, write to us, or create an account.
          </li>
          <li>
            An account syncs your notebook locked with a key we never have, so
            we can’t read it. It keeps no name, no email address, and no
            record of where you connect from.
          </li>
          <li>
            There are no ads, we don’t track you across sites, and nothing is
            sold. Two pages show other companies’ players or a giving form,
            which follow their own privacy policies (see
            <a href="#privacy-external-links">External links</a>).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-scope">
        <h2 id="privacy-scope">Scope</h2>
        <p>
          This policy applies to the LIT Bible website at litbible.net, the
          companion iOS and Android apps, and the optional LIT Bible account
          that syncs a notebook between them. The LIT Bible is a project of
          the Liberating Scripture Collective, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit. The
          sections labeled “(app)” describe
          how the apps handle data. Where a detail is platform-specific (for
          example, iCloud sync on iOS or automatic Google Drive backup on
          Android), it is labeled <em>On iOS</em> or <em>On Android</em>;
          details that apply to both are labeled <em>On both platforms</em>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-collect">
        <h2 id="privacy-collect">What we collect</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Website:</strong> The site collects personal information only
          when you choose to give it:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Mailing list:</strong> If you subscribe, your email address
            is stored by Brevo, which manages the mailing list solely to send
            LIT Bible updates. You can unsubscribe anytime using links in
            emails.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Contact and app support forms:</strong> Messages sent
            through the contact form or the app support form (name, email,
            message, and on the app support form, which app you are using)
            are delivered directly to our inbox by Cloudflare, the site’s
            hosting provider. No separate form-processing service handles
            them. We use what you send only to respond to you; it is never
            sold or shared.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>An account:</strong> if you create one, it keeps what
            <a href="#privacy-accounts">Accounts and sync</a>, below,
            describes, and no name or email address.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The contact and subscribe forms, and “Create an account” on the
          website, are protected from spam by Cloudflare Turnstile, a
          privacy-preserving alternative to CAPTCHAs that does not track you across sites. On the
          subscribe form, Turnstile runs in “invisible” mode: it verifies you
          silently in the background, with nothing to click or solve. When you
          create an account, the notebook service asks Cloudflare to confirm
          the check, passing along the address your request came from, as
          Cloudflare asks; the service keeps neither. See Cloudflare’s{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/en-gb/turnstile-privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Turnstile Privacy Policy Addendum</a>{" "}
          for how Turnstile itself handles data during that check.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cookies and browser storage:</strong> The site sets one
          cookie of its own, a small flag (<code>lit_apps_launch_v1</code>)
          that remembers you have closed the announcement of the LIT Bible
          apps, so it doesn’t reappear. It contains no personal data and
          expires after 365 days. A few settings are kept in your browser’s
          local storage and never leave your device: your display settings
          (font, theme, text size, line spacing, justification, and what the page shows),
          whether you have turned keyboard shortcuts off, the place you last reached in
          each book in Read View, the chapter you last read (so the site
          can offer to take you back to it), and whether you have closed the
          Android app banner (which comes back after 90 days). A count of the pages you’ve viewed in the current tab is
          kept in session storage, which your browser clears when the tab
          closes, so the app announcement can wait until your second page.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The Study Desk preview:</strong> Readers who switch on the Study Desk,
          a preview of study tools for computers, can keep notes, highlights,
          bookmarks, labels, sheets, and collections. These are stored in your
          browser’s IndexedDB on your computer. Unless you sync them with an
          account (<a href="#privacy-accounts">below</a>), nothing about them
          is sent to the LIT Bible team or anywhere else. A backup you make
          from the notebook page is a file on your own computer. You can
          remove everything with the notebook page’s “Delete in bulk” buttons,
          or by clearing this site’s data in your browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Study Desk’s settings are kept in local storage under these
          names:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lit-desk-preview</code>: whether you switched the preview on.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-account</code>: whether you opened accounts in this browser while they are being tested.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-account-last</code>: whether the header showed your account button the last time, so the next page can leave the right room for it.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-panel</code>: whether the Notebook is open or closed.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-tab</code>: which of the Notebook’s tabs you chose last.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-min</code>: whether the Notebook is minimized.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-start</code>: how the panel opens on each page.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-show</code>: an older note that the Display menu’s “Show the Notebook” was off. That switch is gone, so the note is removed the next time you read a chapter with Study on.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-notes</code>: whether your notes show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-note-font</code>: which font your notes are shown in.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-bookmarks</code>: whether your bookmarks show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-highlights</code>: whether your highlights show.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-read-highlights</code>: whether your highlights show in Read View.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-hide</code>: while “Your marks” is off, which of your notes, highlights, and bookmarks were showing, so turning it on shows them again.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-home</code>: where you chose to keep the notebook.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-remind</code>: when you asked to be reminded to back up your notebook again, if you chose “Remind me tomorrow” or “Not this week.”</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-tour</code>: which steps of the notebook tour you have seen, and your answer when it was offered.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-page-tour</code>: the same for the Study Desk tour.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-sheet-tour</code>: the same for the Sheets tour.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-print-ask</code>: a moment’s note that you pressed Print, for the tab that prints.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-lexicon</code>: which lexicon you chose for the Greek tab.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-greek-notes</code>: whether Greek searches list your notes, once you turn that off.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-reading-off</code>: an older note of which display settings the Reading layout had turned off. It is read once, to turn them back on, and then removed.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-layout</code>: how you arranged the panes on the Study Desk page, your default arrangement, the passages its panes show, which sheet each sheet pane shows, and what each LIT pane’s “Show” has turned off.</li>
          <li><code>lit-sheet-paper</code>: whether you print sheets on US Letter or A4, once you choose.</li>
          <li><code>lit-greek-commands</code>: that the window listing the Greek search commands has opened once, so it doesn’t open by itself again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Session storage holds these until the tab closes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>lit-desk-measure</code>: how wide you dragged the text column.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-width</code>: how wide you dragged the notebook.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-float-width</code>: how wide you dragged the notebook where it lies over the text.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-greek-split</code>: how you divided the Greek tab between the text and a word’s entry.</li>
          <li><code>lit-greek-commands-pos</code>: where you dragged the Greek search commands window.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-follow</code>: which bookmark is following you in this tab, once you start it following (from your list, its menu, or its dialog), so it moves with you while you read on in that book (held until you stop it or the tab closes, and let go after 12 hours away from that book).</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-release-feed</code>: a copy of the release notes, so a page doesn’t download them twice.</li>
          <li><code>lit-desk-note-draft</code>: the words of a note you are writing, so a reload doesn’t lose them.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The notebook also remembers, in its own storage:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>the date of the newest release note you have looked at, and how many changes it listed, to show what is new in your verses;</li>
          <li>the date you last backed up your notebook;</li>
          <li>which sheets you closed the template’s description on, so it stays closed;</li>
          <li>
            whether it asked your browser to keep the notebook so it isn’t
            cleared when space runs short (some browsers show a prompt for
            that; the notebook asks again after a day if the answer was no,
            or when you press the button in Settings).
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Until accounts open, a browser syncs only if you opened them there
          to test them. A browser that syncs its
          notebook with an account notes in the notebook that it is linked,
          and keeps what syncing needs in a second database beside it,
          <code>lit-desk-sync</code>: that browser’s keys, its sign-in, and
          a note of which entries are in step with the account. An entry
          from another device that can’t be shown yet waits there too,
          encrypted as the account holds it. Nothing else of your notes is
          in that database. The account page keeps four more things there:
          your sign-in, from the moment you sign in until this browser joins
          your notebook or you sign out; a new recovery code, encrypted with
          this browser’s own key, until you have typed two of its groups
          back; which of your devices this browser has already told you
          about; and, where signing in with Apple or Google is offered (it
          is not yet), a note of the sign-in you began, for the five minutes
          it has to finish. So that a page you are reading can tell you
          when a request is waiting on your account, or when a device
          joins your notebook, that database also keeps when it last
          checked and what it has already told you: times and the
          account’s own ids for them, never a device’s name.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your open tabs tell one another about changes, and about a followed
          bookmark or a presented sheet, through your browser’s own tab-to-tab
          messages, which stay on your device. A sheet link someone sends you
          is held in that tab’s history entry while you look at it. The
          notebook is described on its{" "}
          <a href="/desk/help/">help page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is privacy-preserving
          by design: “it does not use cookies, does not track individuals across
          sites or sessions, and does not build behavioral profiles.” Aggregated
          data like page views and general geographic region cannot identify
          individuals. Cloudflare, the hosting provider, may log basic request
          data at the network level for security and performance, subject to{" "}
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Security reports:</strong> Each page of the site is sent
          with a list of the places it may load scripts, images, and other
          files from. When your browser sees a page try to load something
          that isn’t on that list (most often something a browser extension
          added), it tells the site automatically, so we can correct the list
          and notice code that doesn’t belong. From each report we keep only
          a count: the day, the rule involved, where the file would have come
          from, what asked for it, which page it happened on, and the kind of
          browser (such as Firefox or Safari). If a browser extension was
          involved, we keep only that fact, not which extension. Your IP
          address is used only in the moment, to limit how many reports one
          connection can send; we do not store or log it, your browser’s full
          identification, or anything else about you or your device. The
          counts are deleted after 30 days.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>App (on both platforms):</strong> Without an account, the
          app does not collect your name, email address, device identifiers,
          or other identifying information. The apps don’t offer accounts
          yet; when they do, an account in the app works as
          <a href="#privacy-accounts">Accounts and sync</a> describes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-accounts">
        <h2 id="privacy-accounts">Accounts and sync</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Accounts are not open yet.</strong> They are being tested,
          and this section describes how they work, for the few people
          testing them now and for everyone once they open.
        </p>
        <p>
          An account is optional. Reading the LIT, the apps, and the Study
          Desk all work without one. An account keeps your notebook (your
          notes, highlights, bookmarks, and sheets) in step across your
          devices. The LIT Bible team runs it on Cloudflare, the site’s
          hosting provider.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Locked before it leaves your device.</strong> Before any part
          of your notebook is sent, your device locks it with a key that only
          your devices have. We never have that key, so we can’t read your
          notebook, and neither can Cloudflare or anyone who gets into our
          systems. Your devices’ names are locked the same way once a device
          has joined.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Signing in:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>With a passkey,</strong> which your browser, phone, or
            password manager makes and keeps. We keep its public half and its
            id, never anything that could sign in as you. There is no
            password.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>With Apple or Google</strong> (not offered yet). You sign
            in on Apple’s or Google’s own page, under their privacy policies.
            We ask them for no name and no email address, and keep only the
            account id they give us for you. For Sign in with Apple we also
            keep a token Apple issues, which we can read, so that we can tell
            Apple to end the connection when you remove that way in or delete
            your account. It opens nothing in your notebook.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Where your notebook’s key can be opened.</strong> When you
          set up, you choose “Follows my account,” where your passkey can open
          your notebook on a new device, or “This device only,” where only
          your devices and your recovery code can. Under “Follows my account,”
          anyone who can use your passkey can open your notebook, so it is as
          safe as the place your passkey is kept. Either way, a recovery code
          is made for you. We never see it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What the service keeps, and can read.</strong> These are
          what syncing needs:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            the account’s id, and for each way you sign in, a passkey’s public
            key and id, whether it syncs between your devices, and the use
            count it reports, or the account id Apple or Google gives us;
          </li>
          <li>
            each signed-in session: how it signed in, when, and when it ends
            (we keep only a fingerprint of its token);
          </li>
          <li>
            your devices: when each was added and how, and when each last
            synced;
          </li>
          <li>
            for each record in your notebook: its id, when it last changed,
            when it was deleted (for a deletion), and its size to the nearest
            256 bytes;
          </li>
          <li>
            whether a recovery code or a passkey can open your notebook, and
            which device added it;
          </li>
          <li>
            while a new device waits to be approved, for up to ten minutes:
            its kind (iPhone or iPad, Android, or website) and its model, such
            as “iPhone 15” or, on the website, its browser and system, such as
            “Chrome on Windows”;
          </li>
          <li>
            while it waits, a request to delete your account or to start a
            new notebook: when it was made, from what kind of device, and how
            that device signed in.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>What the service keeps, locked so that we can’t read
          it:</strong> every note, highlight, bookmark, sheet, label, and
          anything else in your notebook; your devices’ names; and the copies
          of your notebook’s key that your devices, your passkey, and your
          recovery code open.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we never have:</strong> your notebook’s key, your
          recovery code, anything that would let us use your passkey, your
          name, your email address, a password, your reading history, or your
          settings. The service doesn’t keep or log the address your requests
          come from: Cloudflare counts requests by address for one minute, to
          slow down floods, and that count is all. The service’s own line for
          each request says when, which kind of request, whether it worked,
          and how long it took, and nothing about who asked. Those lines
          aren’t stored: we see them only as they happen, when we watch for a
          fault.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What can still be seen.</strong> Like any website,
          Cloudflare sees the address and time of each request as it passes.
          From what the service keeps, we could tell how many records a
          notebook holds and roughly how large each is, when they change, how
          many devices are signed in, and how you sign in.
        </p>
        <p><strong>How long each thing is kept:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            your account and your notebook’s locked records: until you delete
            them or the account;
          </li>
          <li>
            an account with no notebook in it, because one was never set up
            or was replaced and not set up again: deleted by itself once 30
            days have passed since it was made or replaced and no one is
            signed in to it;
          </li>
          <li>
            a deleted record: a locked copy in your notebook’s trash for 30
            days, so you can restore it; after that, the next of your devices
            to open your notebook replaces it with only a locked note that it
            was deleted, so your other devices delete it too;
          </li>
          <li>
            a session: 30 days from signing in, kept going by use for up to a
            year, after which you sign in again;
          </li>
          <li>
            a sign-in being made: five minutes; a device waiting to be
            approved: ten minutes;
          </li>
          <li>
            a request to delete your account or start a new notebook: seven
            days, then it is carried out, unless it is canceled;
          </li>
          <li>
            request counts by address: one minute;
          </li>
          <li>
            our database’s backup history: up to 30 days, which can hold a
            deleted account until it ages out; the notebook in it stays
            locked.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Deleting your account.</strong> You can delete it from any
          device signed in to it, in the apps or on the website, including on
          our <a href="/delete-account/">Delete your account</a> page with no
          app installed. From a device that holds your notebook’s key, it is
          deleted at once. From a sign-in that hasn’t opened your notebook,
          the request waits seven days, and every device that holds your
          notebook shows it and can cancel it, so that someone who gets into
          your Apple or Google account can’t take your notebook while you look
          away. Deleting removes everything the service holds for the account,
          and if you signed in with Apple, we tell Apple to end the
          connection. Your notebook stays on each of your devices until you
          erase it there. Because we keep no name or email address, we can’t
          find or delete an account for you from a message: only someone
          signed in to it can.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Stopping on one computer.</strong> Signing out, or “Stop
          syncing on this computer,” stops syncing there and keeps the
          notebook in that browser.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Getting your notebook back.</strong> We can’t open your
          notebook, so we can’t recover it for you. Your devices, your
          recovery code, and, under “Follows my account,” your passkey are the
          ways back.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-reading-data">
        <h2 id="privacy-reading-data">Your reading data (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> Everything you create in the app
          (highlights, notes, and your reading position) is stored privately on
          your device. Without an account, the LIT Bible team cannot access
          it. With an account, a locked copy of your notes, highlights, and
          bookmarks is synced as <a href="#privacy-accounts">Accounts and
          sync</a> describes, which we can’t read; your reading position is
          not synced.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS:</strong> If you have iCloud enabled for LIT Bible,
          this data is also synced to your personal iCloud account via Apple’s
          CloudKit. Sync happens automatically in the background, the same way
          Apple Notes and Reminders work. Your data is stored in your own
          private iCloud database; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there
          either. You can turn off iCloud sync for LIT Bible at any time in iOS
          Settings → [Your Name] → iCloud → Apps Using iCloud. App preferences
          like text size and display settings are stored in your device’s
          standard preferences storage and are not synced to iCloud.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android:</strong> If your device has Backup enabled, a copy
          of your highlights and notes is included in your device’s automatic
          backup to your own Google Drive (via Android Auto Backup), so they can
          restore on a new device. This backup lives in your personal Google
          account; the LIT Bible team has no access to it there either. You can
          turn it off at any time in your device’s settings under Settings →
          Google → Backup. Your reading position and your app preferences (like
          text size and display settings) are stored on your device but are not
          included in this backup; only your highlights and notes are.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-content-updates">
        <h2 id="privacy-content-updates">Content updates (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The app periodically checks
          litbible.net servers for Bible translation text updates. During this
          check, your device downloads changed content with no information about
          you, your device, or reading activity transmitted to the servers. The
          process compares file versions and downloads updated text only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-widget">
        <h2 id="privacy-widget">The home screen widget (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> The LIT Bible widget displays
          lectionary readings and liturgical calendar information. All widget
          data is computed on your device from information bundled with the app.
          The widget makes no network requests.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-external-links">
        <h2 id="privacy-external-links">External links</h2>
        <p>
          Two pages also show other companies’ content inside the page: the
          <a href="/found-in-translation-podcast/">podcast page</a> plays the
          latest episodes in YouTube, Spotify, and Apple Podcasts players, and
          the <a href="/support/">Support page</a> shows Give Lively’s giving
          form. Your browser contacts those companies when the player or form
          loads, before you press play or give, and they may set cookies or
          record the visit under their own privacy policies.
        </p>
        <p>
          The website and app include links to external services. Following
          these links subjects you to those third parties’ privacy policies.
          The LIT Bible team does not track whether you visit these pages or
          take actions on them.
        </p>
        <p>External services linked include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Give Lively</strong>, for contributions to the LIT Bible and
            the Liberating Scripture Collective
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Apple Podcasts</strong>, for the Found in Translation
            podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spotify</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>YouTube</strong>, for the Found in Translation podcast
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>CreativeCommons.org</strong>, to display the full license
            text
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>liberatingscripture.org</strong>, the Liberating Scripture
            Collective’s website
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-sharing">
        <h2 id="privacy-sharing">Sharing (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On both platforms:</strong> When you select text and choose
          Share, your device’s standard share sheet opens. The selected passage
          text is passed to whatever app you choose to share it with. The LIT
          Bible team does not log what you share, where you share it, or
          frequency.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-third-party">
        <h2 id="privacy-third-party">Third-party software (app)</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>On iOS,</strong> the app uses one third-party software
          library: <strong>GRDB</strong>, an open-source database library that
          stores scripture content locally on your device. It makes no network
          connections of its own. iCloud sync uses Apple’s CloudKit framework,
          which is part of the iOS operating system and subject to Apple’s
          privacy policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>On Android,</strong> the app uses two third-party software
          libraries: <strong>Room</strong> (an open-source database library from
          Google that stores scripture content locally on your device using the
          device’s built-in SQLite), and <strong>OkHttp</strong> (an open-source
          HTTP library used only for the content-update downloads described
          above, fetching translation text from litbible.net). Automatic backup
          of your highlights and notes uses Android Auto Backup, which is part
          of the Android operating system and subject to Google’s privacy
          policy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-choices">
        <h2 id="privacy-choices">Your choices</h2>
        <p>
          Wherever you live, you can ask what we hold about you, and ask us to
          correct or delete it, by writing to us through the
          <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. We can find what the mailing
          list and your messages hold from your email address, and you can
          leave the mailing list with the link in any of its emails. An
          account keeps no name or email address, so we can’t find it from a
          message: what it holds is shown, and deleted, on any device signed
          in to it.
        </p>
        <p>
          We don’t sell personal information, use it for advertising, or use
          it to build a profile of you. We share it only with the services
          named on this page that do work for us (Cloudflare and Brevo), and
          with Apple or Google if you choose to sign in with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do Not Track:</strong> we don’t track you across other sites
          and show no ads, and the site works the same with the signal on or
          off. The players and the giving form described under
          <a href="#privacy-external-links">External links</a> follow their
          own companies’ policies, including on Do Not Track.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-children">
        <h2 id="privacy-children">Children</h2>
        <p>
          Anyone can read the LIT, use the apps, and keep a notebook on their
          own device; none of that needs an account or asks your age. An
          account is for people 13 and older, or older where the law of your
          country sets a higher age for using an online service without a
          parent’s permission (our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a> say
          so). The website and the apps have no advertising and no social
          features, and they don’t knowingly collect personal information from
          children under 13. A newsletter subscription or a message through
          the contact or app support forms requires you to actively submit it
          and is never required to access content. An account keeps no name,
          email address, or age, so we can’t tell a child’s account from
          anyone else’s, or find one from a message. If a child under 13 has
          made an account, a parent can delete it from any device signed in
          to it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-changes">
        <h2 id="privacy-changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes in a way that matters for your data, we will
          update the effective date above, say what changed at the top of this
          page for at least 30 days, and note it in the apps’ What’s New. We
          can’t email you about it, since we keep no email address unless you
          subscribe to the mailing list.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="privacy-contact">
        <h2 id="privacy-contact">Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about this policy can be directed to the LIT Bible team at
          <a href="/contact">litbible.net/contact</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
